stems/areas such as skin and skeletal assessment. [Data: Relationships (282, 281, 277, 11546, 11548, +more)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Head and neck inspection is represented through nose and ear examination during the physical exam\",\"explanation\":\"The **NOSE** is explicitly characterized as a body area routinely examined during a physical exam, including looking inside the nostrils to assess the nasal passages; the relationship data reiterates this as an examination step performed during PHYSICAL EXAM. [Data: Entities (267); Relationships (282)].\\n\\nSimilarly, **EARS** are described as anatomical sense organs commonly inspected visually during a routine head and neck assessment; the relationship data specifies that clinicians “look in the ears” during the PHYSICAL EXAM as a routine component. [Data: Entities (266); Relationships (281)].\\n\\nBeyond routine inspection, the NOSE entity description also situates the nasal cavity/nasopharynx region as clinically relevant in head and neck evaluation and describes the nose as a common anatomical entry route for flexible scopes used to examine upper aerodigestive structures (e.g., nasolaryngoscopy) with numbing medication sometimes applied to facilitate passage. This adds context to why nose examination may matter clinically even when the core relationship is simply “examined during physical exam.” [Data: Entities (267); Relationships (282)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Baseline measurement in the physical exam includes recording height\",\"explanation\":\"**HEIGHT** is defined as a body measurement routinely taken and recorded during a physical exam, commonly used alongside weight to evaluate overall health and support general assessment. [Data: Entities (262)].\\n\\nThe relationship explicitly ties height measurement to the PHYSICAL EXAM and notes it as part of a patient’s baseline assessment, indicating that this community includes not only inspection-based elements but also standardized quantitative measurements. [Data: Relation